  "registry_fetched": "2026-11-08",
  "rule": {
   "who_decides": "No Connecticut statute sets a sale procedure for a town's surplus fire truck. Conn. Gen. Stat. 7-148(c)(3)(A) gives every municipality the power to 'hold, condemn, lease, sell, manage, transfer, release and convey such real and personal property as the purposes of the municipality ... require.' It names no approving body, no voter approval, and no bid rule, so each town's charter or ordinance decides. In the local rules read, the owning department or board declares the item surplus, a town official or board approves, and the purchasing agent runs the sale: East Hartford needs Town Council approval (Sec. 10-3); Waterford's owning agency, board or commission declares surplus and the purchasing agent disposes, under the board of selectmen's authority (Code 3.08.020(B), Ch. 2.112); Montville's Finance Director and Mayor declare surplus and approve the disposal method. A few towns send even a $1 transfer to a town meeting (Portland, 2024). Fire districts formed under 7-324 to 7-329 are run by a board of directors, but the statute says nothing about selling property; it requires a district vote for any contract over $10,000 (grand list up to $20 million) or $20,000 (larger grand list) a year, which governs buying a new truck, not selling the old one. Volunteer companies that own their trucks as nonprofits decide sales under their own bylaws.",
   "law": "Conn. Gen. Stat. 7-148(c)(3)(A) (municipal power to sell and convey real and personal property); Conn. Gen. Stat. 7-326 to 7-328 (district purposes, meetings, and the 7-328(a) district-vote rule for contracts of $10,000 or $20,000 and up); town charters and ordinances (East Hartford Code Sec. 10-3; Waterford Code 3.08.020(B) and Ch. 2.112; Montville surplus personal property ordinance).",
   "url": "https://prdext3.cga.ct.gov/current/pub/chap_098.htm",
   "thresholds": [
    {
     "below": null,
     "rule": "State law sets no dollar line, approving body or bid rule for a town selling a truck (7-148(c)(3)(A)); the town charter or ordinance decides. For example, East Hartford needs Town Council approval for any sale other than a trade-in; Waterford's purchasing agent runs the disposal once the owning board declares the item surplus; Montville's Mayor gives final approval after the Finance Director is told. Most towns then sell at online auction, so the council or board vote is the earliest public signal."
    }
   ],
   "note": "The earliest public signal is a council or selectmen agenda line declaring a vehicle surplus, or a town meeting or referendum notice funding the new truck, often one to three years before delivery. An auction listing is a late signal. Towns may also sell through the state DAS auction contract (22PSX0014)."
  },
