  "registry_fetched": "2026-11-08",
  "rule": {
   "who_decides": "The governing body votes by resolution: the township committee or borough or city council for a municipal fire department, and the Board of Fire Commissioners (five elected commissioners) for a fire district. A fire district sells as a body corporate. A volunteer fire company that owns its own truck sells it by its own vote. No voter referendum is needed to sell; voters decide only on buying (the February fire district election).",
   "law": "N.J.S.A. 40A:11-36 (Local Public Contracts Law, sale of personal property not needed for public use); N.J.S.A. 40A:14-70(b) (fire district power to sell, subject to 40A:12-13 or 40A:12-14); N.J.S.A. 40A:12-21.1 (sale to a volunteer fire company for nominal sum)",
   "url": "https://codes.findlaw.com/nj/title-40a-municipalities-and-counties/nj-st-sect-40a-11-36/",
   "thresholds": [
    {
     "below": 7950,
     "rule": "Fair value at or under 15% of the bid threshold: $7,950 for a unit with a qualified purchasing agent (bid threshold $53,000), $2,625 without one (bid threshold $17,500), the figures in force since 1 July 2025. No public sale is needed; the unit may sell privately by resolution. Few trucks fall here except scrap."
    },
    {
     "below": null,
     "rule": "Above 15% of the bid threshold, 40A:11-36(1) requires a public sale to the highest bidder, by sealed bid or public auction, at any higher value. Notice runs in the official newspaper and the sale is held 7 to 14 days after the last notice. Units without a purchasing agent reach this rule at $2,625. The unit may reject all bids and re-advertise; after a second rejection it may sell privately, but not below the highest rejected bid. If no bids come, it may sell privately at no less than fair value. No advertising is needed when the buyer is the U.S., New Jersey or another government, and under 40A:12-21.1 a town may give a truck to a volunteer fire company for a nominal sum."
    }
   ],
   "note": "Most New Jersey units sell at public online auction, so the resolution that authorizes the sale is the earliest public signal. The dollar lines are those in force since 1 July 2025 (DCA Local Finance Notice 2025-08). A bill introduced in February 2026 (S3437) would redraw the bands; it is not law."
