 {
  "code": "PA",
  "name": "Pennsylvania",
  "departments": 1771,
  "leads": {
   "week": 5,
   "month": 35,
   "watch": 35,
   "total": 75
  },
  "by_kind": {
   "new_truck_ordered": 28,
   "grant": 27,
   "sale_planned": 8,
   "new_truck_arrived": 4,
   "delivery_due": 4,
   "council_approval": 4
  },
  "coverage": "deep",
  "layers": [
   "grants",
   "trade_press",
   "notices"
  ],
  "registry_fetched": "2026-11-08",
  "rule": {
   "who_decides": "Borough: borough council, by resolution (8 Pa.C.S. 1201.2(a)). Second-class township: board of supervisors approves any sale of township personal property (53 P.S. 66504(a), Second Class Township Code section 1504). First-class township: board of commissioners (73 Pa.C.S. 4303(a); the First Class Township Code of 1931 was repealed and moved into Title 73 by Act 7 of 2026, in force about April 12, 2026). Third-class city: city council, by resolution (11 Pa.C.S. 12402.1(c)(1)). Voters do not vote on a truck sale. A volunteer fire company that owns its truck is a private nonprofit: its own board or members decide under its bylaws, and none of the municipal codes bind it. Where the municipality owns the truck (common in boroughs and cities, and some townships), the governing body must act, and its sale to a volunteer fire company or another government skips advertising and highest-bidder rules.",
   "law": "Borough Code, 8 Pa.C.S. 1201.2 (personal property, as amended by Act 150 of 2018) and 1201.3 (exceptions). Second Class Township Code, Act of May 1, 1933, P.L. 103, No. 69, section 1504, 53 P.S. 66504 (section amended by Act 30 of 2012; subsections (a) and (b) amended by Act 21 of 2017, P.L. 302, which set the $2,000 figure). First-class townships: 73 Pa.C.S. 4303 (personal property) and 4304 (exceptions), added by Act 7 of 2026 (Feb. 11, 2026, P.L. 19), which repealed the First Class Township Code (Act 331 of 1931, 53 P.S. 55101 et seq.) and says the new text continues the old code without change of meaning. Third Class City Code, 11 Pa.C.S. 12402.1(c)-(f) (subsection (c) amended by Act 150 of 2018). Companion act for no-bid sales: Act of Oct. 27, 1979, P.L. 241, No. 78. Cities of other classes (Philadelphia, Pittsburgh, Scranton) and home-rule municipalities follow their own charters.",
   "url": "https://www.palegis.us/statutes/consolidated/view-statute?txtType=PDF&ttl=08&div=0&chapter=12&section=1&subsctn=2",
   "thresholds": [
    {
     "below": 2000,
     "rule": "All four codes use $2,000. Borough (8 Pa.C.S. 1201.2(b)), first-class township (73 Pa.C.S. 4303(d)) and second-class township (53 P.S. 66504(b)): for items or lots with estimated fair market value under $2,000, the governing body adopts a resolution setting a sale procedure, and sales under it need no further vote. Third-class city (11 Pa.C.S. 12402.1(c)(3)): under $2,000, council may sell in whole or in part for the best price obtainable, after approving the disposal by resolution."
    },
    {
     "below": null,
     "rule": "At or above $2,000 (all four codes): the governing body approves the sale (borough and city by resolution), estimates the value of the whole lot, and advertises it once in a newspaper of general circulation at least 10 days before bids open or the auction runs. Online auctions are allowed; the ad gives the web address, date, time and duration. Award goes to the best responsible bidder (borough, city) or highest bidder (townships). The body may reject bids it thinks are under fair market value. If no bids come, the 1979 no-bid act applies (second-class townships may also adopt a sale procedure after two failed attempts)."
    },
    {
     "below": null,
     "rule": "Exceptions (any value): no advertising or highest-bidder rule applies to a sale to the federal or state government, a county, city, borough, town, township, school district, institution district, authority or council of governments, or to a volunteer fire company, volunteer ambulance service or rescue squad. Location limits differ: borough (1201.3(a)(1)) and first-class township (4304(a)(1)) list them with 'located within the borough/township'; second-class township (1504(c)(2)(ix)) lists 'a volunteer fire company' with no location limit and limits only ambulance and rescue squads to those within the township; third-class city (12402.1(e)(2)) allows one 'located within the city or providing emergency services in the city'. A trade-in or exchange for replacement property is also exempt in all four codes (borough 1201.2(c) and first-class township 4303(e) still need a resolution)."
    }
   ],
   "note": "A sale to a volunteer fire company or another government skips bidding, so a municipal truck often moves quietly (Harrisburg to Highspire, 2025). At $2,000 and up the sale must be advertised, so the first public signal is usually a council or supervisors minute authorizing the sale, then an ad or online listing 10 or more days before the close. Companies that own their trucks often post the sale on their own site or Facebook first. Second-class townships moved from $1,000 to $2,000 in 2017; older resolutions that cite $1,000 are out of date."
  },
